                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_leak_right:    `<strong>That's the leak.</strong> The 5–7 year replacement cycle is where durable tools are discarded early and repairs are missed — ecological and social value lost. Everything that depends on re-purchase is at risk from the durability rules.`,
                    coach_r1_leak_wrong:    `<strong>You're looking at a symptom.</strong> Packaging, energy and marketing matter, but they're tweaks. The model's value destruction lives in the replacement cycle itself — that's what the regulation attacks.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named both the leak (the replacement cycle) and what's obsolete (the replacement logic). Now you can choose patterns that change the logic, not decorate it.`,
                    coach_r1_boltonrisk:    `<strong>Careful — this is how greenwashing starts.</strong> Low willingness to redesign the model means you'll add a take-back bin and call it a transformation. Lüdeke-Freund's patterns change the logic, not the packaging.`,
                    coach_r2_coherent:      `<strong>A coherent combination.</strong> You spanned maintaining, unlocking and sharing value — the patterns reinforce each other. This is how the framework is meant to be used.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>Reasonable, but narrow.</strong> Two value functions reinforce; adding the third (sharing or unlocking) would let the patterns compound rather than overlap.`,
                    coach_r2_narrow:        `<strong>All eggs in one basket.</strong> Your patterns all perform the same value function — they overlap instead of reinforcing. Spread across maintaining, unlocking and sharing.`,
                    coach_r2_conflict:      `<strong>These patterns fight each other.</strong> Keeping the sell-more-units push alongside access/leasing cannibalises both and confuses your trade partners. Patterns combine — but not all combinations cohere. Let the unit-sales push go.`,
                    coach_r2_untagged:      `<strong>You chose by appeal.</strong> If you can't say whether each pattern maintains, unlocks or shares value, you can't tell whether they reinforce or conflict. Re-tag each one before you build the revenue model on them.`,
                    coach_r2_nomaintain:    `<strong>No maintaining pattern.</strong> Helvar's real asset is 15-year durability — without repair, reman or modular design you leave that advantage on the table.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid captures value on two fronts.</strong> Sales fund the transition while phased leasing and refurbished resale build circular revenue — impact rises and the margin floor holds.`,
                    coach_r3_leasing:       `<strong>The right instinct, the wrong pace.</strong> A full subscription switch takes on inventory and financing risk all at once — EBIT just dropped well below 10%. The pattern is ecologically strong but it breaks the company. Phase it.`,
                    coach_r3_payperuse:     `<strong>Pay-per-use is capital-hungry early.</strong> Metering and ownership retention compress margin before usage revenue scales. Strong circularity, but watch the floor.`,
                    coach_r3_resale:        `<strong>Refurbished-resale is margin-friendly and circular.</strong> It extends life and recovers materials while protecting EBIT — a solid backbone, especially paired with repair.`,
                    coach_r3_sale:          `<strong>That's the bolt-on.</strong> Selling the same tools with a repair leaflet protects margin and produces almost no ecological or social value. You've passed the margin test and failed the mandate.`,
                    coach_r3_starved:       `<strong>Impact on paper only.</strong> You chose a circular revenue model but starved reverse logistics and refurbishment — there's no capacity to actually close the loop or create the repair jobs. Fund the operations behind the pattern.`,
                    coach_r3_underspend:    `<strong>Timid.</strong> You've left most of the €24M unspent. A transformation needs resourcing — under-investing now means the patterns never reach scale.`,
                    coach_r4_phased:        `<strong>Phasing is the answer.</strong> Protecting the floor early and ramping impact as refurbished revenue builds shows the board that economic viability and sustainability move together, not against each other.`,
                    coach_r4_bigbang:       `<strong>Bold but exposed.</strong> Switching everything at once delivers impact fast but dips the margin — the board will flinch. Sequencing would have bought you the same impact without the scare.`,
                    coach_r4_slow:          `<strong>You've protected 12% and changed almost nothing.</strong> The investor asked for avoided-waste and repair-jobs numbers and you have none. Margin-safe tokenism passes the floor and fails the mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_promise:       `<strong>A promise isn't a margin bridge.</strong> The owners set a hard 10% floor; they need to see the refurbished-unit economics that hold it, not your conviction that it works out.`,
                    coach_r4_retreat:       `<strong>You blinked.</strong> Retreating to unit sales with a green claim keeps 12% and abandons the transformation — exactly the greenwashing the owners forbade. The service market is ceded to ToolFlow.`,
                    coach_r4_story:         `<strong>The story is good — now quantify it.</strong> Your model holds margin and the patterns cohere, but the investor wants measured impact. Attach the numbers and the buy-in is yours.`,
                    coach_r4_ecoonly:       `<strong>Two legs of a three-legged stool.</strong> Carbon figures alone leave the social dimension blank. Repair networks create skilled jobs; access lets small trades afford tools they couldn't buy. Where does a person benefit, not just the planet and the P&L?`,

